---
title: Bundle Project
sidebar:
  order: 11
---

Use `Bundle project` to package related NAHPU data and files for exchange,
publication, archiving, or reproducibility. The bundle screen previews the
exact files and table fields before writing anything.

NAHPU provides three bundle types:

- **Darwin Core Archive** for established biodiversity publishing systems that
  expect `meta.xml`.
- **Darwin Core Data Package** for relational Darwin Core data described by a
  Frictionless `datapackage.json`.
- **NAHPU Data Package** for a reproducible copy of the complete NAHPU database,
  configuration, and available user files.

## Create a bundle

{% steps %}

1. Navigate to the `Dashboard` page.
2. Open the project menu and select `Bundle project`.
3. Select the `Bundle format`.
4. Select the `Archive format` when the chosen bundle supports more than one
   container.
   - `TAR.GZ` is the default for Data Packages.
   - `ZIP` is available for tools and operating systems that prefer standard
     ZIP archives.
   - Darwin Core Archive always uses ZIP.
5. For a Darwin Core bundle, choose `All taxa` or `Selected taxa`.
   Selecting Mammals also includes Bats.
6. Add a `File name` and select the destination `Directory`.
7. Review `Package contents`. Expand a CSV resource to see its exported
   fields.
8. Review any warnings, especially missing media or a DwC-DP ZIP compatibility
   notice.
9. Click `Export`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="caution" title="Review before sharing" %}
NAHPU Data Packages contain the complete NAHPU database and user
configuration. They can include personnel details, project locations, media,
custom fonts, and records from projects other than the currently open project.
Treat them as potentially sensitive files.
{% /aside %}

## Choose an archive container

### TAR.GZ

`TAR.GZ` first combines package files into a tar archive and then compresses
that stream with gzip. It is the default for Darwin Core Data Packages and
NAHPU Data Packages.

Typical file names are:

- `specimens.dwc-dp.tar.gz`
- `nahpu-data.nahpu-dp.tar.gz`

After extraction, `datapackage.json` and any package-specific metadata are at
the root of the extracted package.

### ZIP

ZIP stores and compresses multiple files in one widely supported archive.
NAHPU Data Package supports ZIP as a normal container. Darwin Core Data
Package supports ZIP as a compatibility option, but TAR.GZ is the
standards-oriented choice under the current DwC-DP compression guidance.

Darwin Core Archive always uses ZIP because ZIP is part of its normal exchange
workflow.

## Deep dive

### Darwin Core Archive

A Darwin Core Archive is a specimen-focused exchange package based on the
[Darwin Core Text Guidelines](https://dwc.tdwg.org/text/).

#### Scope

The selected taxon groups determine the specimen occurrences in the archive.
NAHPU then follows relationships from those specimens to include available:

- collecting events;
- specimen parts and other material entities;
- measurements;
- media;
- collectors, catalogers, preparators, and media creators.

Empty optional fields and empty extension tables are omitted.

#### Root files

{% filetree %}
- meta.xml
- eml.xml
- occurrence.csv
- material.csv
- measurement_or_fact.csv
- multimedia.csv
- media/
{% /filetree %}

Only files supported by the selected data are written.

`meta.xml` describes the occurrence core and extension files, their column
positions, and their Darwin Core term identifiers. `eml.xml` contains
dataset-level metadata. `occurrence.csv` is always the core table.

#### Relationships

Extension records reference the occurrence core. For example:

- a specimen part becomes a material record linked by occurrence identifier;
- measurements become MeasurementOrFact records;
- media rows reference both the occurrence and the packaged media path.

#### Output

Darwin Core Archive produces:

```text
<file-name>.dwca.zip
```

Choose this format when the receiving repository, collection-management
system, or publishing workflow explicitly expects a Darwin Core Archive.

### Darwin Core Data Package

A Darwin Core Data Package, or DwC-DP, represents Darwin Core data as related
tables under the [Darwin Core Data Package specification](https://dwc.tdwg.org/dp/).
It also follows the Frictionless Data Package model.

#### Root files

{% filetree %}
- datapackage.json
- eml.xml
- occurrence.csv
- event.csv
- material.csv
- occurrence-assertion.csv
- media.csv
- agent.csv
- occurrence-agent-role.csv
- event-agent-role.csv
- material-agent-role.csv
- media-agent-role.csv
- occurrence-media.csv
- media/
{% /filetree %}

Optional tables appear only when data exists.

#### Descriptor

`datapackage.json` identifies the versioned DwC-DP profile and describes:

- every CSV resource;
- CSV media type and format;
- ordered field descriptors;
- primary keys;
- foreign keys;
- relationship predicates.

DwC-DP uses a normalized relational model. Internal primary and foreign key
columns preserve links even where the original human-readable identifier is
also retained.

#### Data mapping

NAHPU maps the selected specimen data into concepts such as:

- `occurrence` for the specimen occurrence;
- `event` for collecting activity and location context;
- `material` for specimen parts;
- `occurrence-assertion` for measurements;
- `agent` and role tables for people and their roles;
- `media` and `occurrence-media` for media metadata and relationships.

#### Archive options

- `TAR.GZ` produces `<file-name>.dwc-dp.tar.gz` and is the default.
- `ZIP` produces `<file-name>.dwc-dp.zip` for compatibility.

When ZIP is selected, NAHPU shows a warning because the current DwC-DP guide
specifies gzip for whole-package compression.

Choose DwC-DP when the recipient supports Frictionless descriptors and needs
explicit table schemas and relational links.

### NAHPU Data Package

NAHPU Data Package is NAHPU's reproducibility and complete-data exchange
format. It is a Frictionless Data Package with additional NAHPU metadata.

Unlike the Darwin Core formats, it is not restricted to selected specimen
taxa. It includes the complete NAHPU application database snapshot.

#### Root layout

{% filetree %}
- datapackage.json
- nahpu.toml
- database/
  - nahpu.sqlite3
- tables/
  - project.csv
  - site.csv
  - coordinate.csv
  - collEvent.csv
  - weather.csv
  - collPersonnel.csv
  - collEffort.csv
  - narrative.csv
  - media.csv
  - narrativeMedia.csv
  - siteMedia.csv
  - specimenMedia.csv
  - associatedData.csv
  - personnelList.csv
  - personnel.csv
  - taxonomy.csv
  - specimen.csv
  - mammalMeasurement.csv
  - avianMeasurement.csv
  - herpMeasurement.csv
  - specimenPart.csv
- configs/
  - user_configs.json
- mappings/
  - sqlite_enums.csv
- vocabularies/
  - site.csv
  - events.csv
  - specimens.csv
- files/
  - ...
{% /filetree %}

All 21 NAHPU tables are included. An empty table is still represented by a CSV
file with its complete header and schema.

#### SQLite snapshot

`database/nahpu.sqlite3` is a consistent, lossless snapshot of the NAHPU
database at export time. It is the authoritative resource for exact values,
including distinctions that a CSV mirror cannot always preserve, such as SQL
`NULL` versus an intentionally empty string.

The CSV files are open, tool-independent representations for inspection,
analysis, and Frictionless-compatible processing.

#### SQLite enum mappings

`mappings/sqlite_enums.csv` explains integer values that NAHPU stores as enum
indexes. Each row includes:

- the SQLite table and column;
- the Dart enum type or logical indexed type;
- the zero-based SQLite integer;
- the stable enum name used in code;
- the human-readable name shown in NAHPU.

Table and column context are included because more than one record type can
use an enum with the same short name but a different value order. The mapping
covers specimen sex and identification confidence, mammal age and reproductive
fields, avian reproductive and molt fields, herpetofauna age, and
echolocation categories. Boolean integers and numeric measurements are not
enum indexes and are therefore not listed.

#### Controlled vocabularies

The package snapshots the current user-controlled type names from NAHPU
Settings into three CSV resources:

- `vocabularies/site.csv` contains site types and habitat types;
- `vocabularies/events.csv` contains collecting methods and collecting
  personnel roles;
- `vocabularies/specimens.csv` contains specimen types and specimen
  treatments.

Each row records the user configuration key, vocabulary name, zero-based list
position, and configured value. If a setting has not yet been customized, the
CSV contains the NAHPU default vocabulary that applies at export time. These
files make the labels referenced by table records directly available to CSV
and Frictionless tools without requiring them to parse the full configuration
document.

#### Frictionless descriptor

`datapackage.json` uses the standard `data-package` profile. Each table is a
`tabular-data-resource` with:

- column order;
- field data types;
- required-field constraints;
- primary keys;
- foreign-key references;
- UTF-8 CSV encoding.

The descriptor also declares the enum mapping and three controlled-vocabulary
CSVs as tabular resources with field schemas and composite primary keys. It
lists the SQLite database, user configuration, manifest, and packaged user
files as non-tabular resources.

#### Reproducibility manifest

`nahpu.toml` records:

- package format name and version;
- export timestamp;
- application name, version, and build number;
- NAHPU database schema version;
- user configuration schema version;
- the number of exported tables;
- the enum-mapping and controlled-vocabulary resource paths and counts;
- the versions of the compiled `nahpu_api` crates, including `nahpu_dp`,
  `nahpu_dwc`, `nahpu_db`, and `nahpu_configs`.

This metadata helps determine which version of NAHPU and its Rust components
created the package.

#### User configuration

`configs/user_configs.json` contains the versioned NAHPU configuration export:

- general user configuration values;
- record export presets;
- document template presets;
- document layouts.

#### User files

When available, NAHPU includes:

- project media;
- personnel media;
- custom user fonts.

Packaged files keep safe relative paths below `files/`. Missing files produce
warnings and are not silently represented as successfully bundled content.

#### Archive options

- `TAR.GZ` produces `<file-name>.nahpu-dp.tar.gz`.
- `ZIP` produces `<file-name>.nahpu-dp.zip`.

The internal package contents are the same in both containers.

## Bundle versus backup

Darwin Core bundles are exchange formats and are not restorable NAHPU
backups.

A NAHPU Data Package contains a restorable database snapshot and configuration,
but its first purpose is a documented, interoperable package. Continue using
[Backup database](./#backup-database) for the existing streamlined
backup and replacement workflow.

## Package validation and warnings

Before writing, NAHPU validates required data and package relationships. After
writing, it reopens the archive and checks that required root metadata exists.

Warnings can include:

- linked media that no longer exists on the device;
- ZIP compatibility mode for DwC-DP.

A warning does not necessarily prevent export, but it should be reviewed before
the package is shared or deposited.
